    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_331'), 'A lesson comment on https://app.codedev.test is rendered with element.innerHTML = markdownToHtml(comment.body). The page has no Content-Security-Policy header. Cookies: sid with HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict. POST /settings/email requires the header X-CSRF-Token, whose value the page reads from <meta name="csrf" content="...">.

Stored comment:
<img src="x" onerror="fetch(''/settings/email'', { method: ''POST'', headers: { ''X-CSRF-Token'': document.querySelector(''meta[name=csrf]'').content, ''Content-Type'': ''application/json'' }, body: JSON.stringify({ email: ''owner@free-certs.example'' }) })">

Statement: "Because sid is HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict and the endpoint checks a CSRF token, the injected script cannot change the email of a learner who views the comment."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The script runs inside https://app.codedev.test, so its fetch is a same-origin request: SameSite does not apply, the browser attaches sid as it does for the app''s own requests, and the script reads the CSRF token from the page like the app does. HttpOnly only stops the script from reading the cookie''s value; it does not stop the browser from sending it. CSRF defences protect against other sites, not against code running on your own origin. The misconception is that HttpOnly makes XSS harmless: the attacker cannot copy the session, but can act as the learner while the page is open, and changing the email is enough to take over the account through a password reset.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_331') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
