WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_411'), 'POST /sessions/revoke-all (14:02) ran:

UPDATE refresh_tokens SET revoked_at = now()
 WHERE user_id = ''u_5521'' AND revoked_at IS NULL;   -- 3 rows

The access guard only verifies the signature and exp. API log afterwards:

14:06:40 GET /courses/42/progress  200  sid=fam_812 iat=14:01:30 exp=14:16:30
14:12:10 GET /me/certificates       200  sid=fam_812 iat=14:01:30 exp=14:16:30
14:16:40 GET /me/certificates       401  token_expired
14:16:41 POST /auth/refresh         401  refresh_token_revoked

Statement: "The laptop kept access until 14:16 because revoking the refresh-token rows does nothing to an access token that was already issued; to stop it earlier, the guard has to check something per request, such as the user''s session version or a deny list of revoked families."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. An access token is self-contained: the guard checked the signature and exp, both fine until 14:16:30, and never looked at refresh_tokens. Revoking the rows only blocked the next refresh, which is exactly what the 14:16:41 line shows. To cut those 14 minutes, the guard needs a cheap per-request check: the token''s ver claim against users.session_version (bumped by sign-out-all), or its sid against a deny list of revoked families. The misconception is that revoking a session in the database revokes the tokens already handed out.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_411') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
