WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_411'), 'Access tokens live 15 minutes, refresh tokens 14 days. Sign out this device does:

UPDATE refresh_tokens SET revoked_at = now() WHERE family_id = $1 AND revoked_at IS NULL;
await redis.set(`deny:${familyId}`, "1", { PX: DENY_TTL_MS });

and the access guard rejects a token when deny:<sid> exists. A reviewer proposes:

const DENY_TTL_MS = 14 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;   // as long as the refresh token could live

Statement: "DENY_TTL_MS must be 14 days; with a shorter entry, an access token of the revoked family could start working again before the family''s refresh token would have expired."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. After the UPDATE, the family can never refresh again, so no new access token is issued for it. Every access token it already has was issued before the revocation and expires at most 15 minutes later. An entry that lives 15 minutes (the access token lifetime, plus your allowed clock skew) covers all of them; after that the exp check rejects them by itself. Keeping entries for 14 days only makes the deny list grow with every sign-out, which is the cost you wanted to bound. The misconception is to tie the deny list to the refresh token lifetime instead of the access token lifetime.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nas_411') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
